  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nBeautiful conditions this afternoon; fair weather cumulus and \ntemperatures in the 70s. This is courtesy of a trough departing the \nNortheast US with expansive high pressure at the surface. This \nsurface high shifts east over the next 24-48 hours as vast \nridging over the Rockies builds in. Southerly return flow, and \nrising heights aloft, permit temperatures to steadily warm in \nsubsequent days. We cannot completely rule out stray showers or \nstorms Saturday afternoon (20% chance) as the western US ridge \nspills into western Indiana and even central Michigan this\nweekend. There some some indications of possible severe storms \nSunday evening as well amid a highly unstable environment, \nincreasing shear, and approaching 70-knot 500mb jet.\n\nA similar setup brews Monday night into Tuesday as an upper-level \nlow drops over the upper Great Lakes. Recent slight-risk severe \nweather days have been highly conditional, and this one appears no \ndifferent. As a result, details are sparse. As mentioned in the\nprevious discussion, hot and humid conditions will return by \nMonday with highs in the 90s (near-90 Sunday) and dew points in \nthe 70s. Beyond this system, energetic northwest flow continues \nthrough the Midwest on the fringe of an upper-elvel ridge now over\nthe Four Corners region. However, this flow will need to \ncompete with eastern Great Lakes high pressure.",
